abstract = "Scholars have long noted a sex-fear of crime paradox, where women, who generally have lower victimization risk, have much higher fear of crime than men. One explanation for these differences is perceptually contemporaneous offenses (Warr 1984) that link fear of one crime, like sexual assault, to a variety of perceptually related crimes. When fear of rape is included in fear models the sex-gap is generally reduced. Recent work in the area, that involved disaggregating samples by sex, has found that the notion of perceptually contemporaneous offenses may apply to men as well as women. Few studies have combined these related lines of inquiry and tested fear of rape and fear of assault in the same model. This article will use data from a 2010 Internet survey to test whether “fear of rape” or “fear of assault” better explain the sex-gap between male and female fear levels, and which crimes serve as “master offenses” for men and women.",
