volume =  33,
number =  1,
pages = "81-87",
abstract = "Research has not investigated how multiple incidents of sexually harassing events impact individuals' stress levels. This research investigated how both victims and uninvolved observers perceive multiple instances of sexual harassment. We used C. E. Seta and Seta's (1992) averaging/summation model as a theoretical basis for making predictions and, consistent with this model, the mean stress rating for participants who thought about a mildly sexually harassing event in addition to a highly sexually harassing one was lower than the mean stress rating for those who thought about a single highly harassing event—an averaging effect. In Experiment 2, conceptually identical results were obtained for observers. We also obtained evidence for summation. Implications of these results for stress and legal cases were discussed.",
