abstract = "The aim of the study was to examine the cognitive processes involved in lay people's health risk assessment using a type of analysis-of-variance approach proposed by Slovic, Fischhoff and Lichtenstein (1986). Participants were presented with a set of scenarios depicting potential hazards as a function of critical characteristics, previously identified in earlier studies, and asked to assess the overall severity of these hazards. The scenarios described the characteristics of one substance in terms of (a) the dread of the health risk it can pose, (b) the number of possible affected people, and (c) scientific experts' knowledge level of the risk posed by this substance. It was shown that averaging was the algebraic structure of the rule used to assess overall severity. One possible explanation of this unusual way of processing information would be that, when judging a risk level, participants were mainly considering what could happen to them personally, without taking into account that the same hazardous consequences could affect other people's health as well.",
