volume =  18,
number =  7,
pages = "669-688",
abstract = "Abstract The first contact many victims and witnesses have with the police is by phoning the police and talking to a call-handler about the incident. This paper provides a descriptive analysis of 40 phone calls from an English police call centre. It discusses questions types used, charts the flow of perpetrator-related information from the caller's account to the call-handlers' written record of the call, examines the use of call-scripts and compares characteristics of victim and witness calls. Only 2.46\% of all questions asked were open, but a total of 88.5\% of questions were nevertheless classified as productive questions (including wh-questions, and specific yes/no questions); 11.5\% questions were classed as unproductive (e.g. leading, suggestive, force-choice). In the call-logs written by call-handlers 21.6\% of perpetrator details mentioned by the callers were missing, 5\% of details had been changed, and 3\% had been erroneously added. In 42.5\% of calls call-handlers used call-scripts, which were associated with fewer overall questions, fewer unproductive questions, but the same number of perpetrator details. Victim calls contained more questions than witness calls. Victims were asked more productive questions but not more unproductive questions. Victims provided the same number of perpetrator details as witnesses.",
month =  aug,
year =  2012,
url = "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/1068316X.2010.534478",
file = "All_Papers/Published_Journal/J_TF1505/Leeney2012_12616.pdf",
