abstract = "Assessed the prevalence of date violence (DV) and rape in adolescents, examined associations with disordered eating behaviors, psychopathology (suicidal thoughts/behavior) and psychological health (emotional well being, self-esteem), and determined if associations were significant after controlling for sociodemographic factors and other physical and sexual abuse by an adult. 81,247 male and female 9th and 12th graders completed the 1998 Minnesota Student Survey. Results show that approximately 9\% of girls and 6\% of boys had experienced DV or rape. There were significant differences across race and grade. DV or rape was associated with higher rates of disordered eating behaviors and suicidal thoughts and attempts, and lower scores on measures of emotional well-being and self-esteem. Over 50\% of youth reporting both DV and rape also reported attempting suicide. Adolescents who experienced both DV and rape were more likely to use laxatives, vomit, use diet pills, binge eat, and have suicidal thoughts or attempts than nonabused peers. These odds were weakened but remained significant after controlling for other abuse. A greater percentage of those who reported an abusive dating experience also reported repeat victimization, compared to peers without an abusive dating experience. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2012 APA, all rights reserved)",
