address = "United States",
number = "21/22",
pages = "200-212",
abstract = "While there is disagreement among criminologists as well as others as to the actual extent of crime and the nature of its distribution, the perception of the general public that crime is increasing exponen? tially is a persistent and significant reality. This condition has not abated even though, in the 1970s, the federal government provided extensive financial resources to 4'improve''its first line of defense, the system of criminal justice, in an attempt to deal more efficiently with the perceived problem of crime. Though one can argue that this was a misplaced effort and a waste of resources that could have been better used elsewhere, the fact remains that these policies led both to a greater awareness of the system and to public disillusionment with established methods of criminal justice. As a result, law enforcement was increasingly viewed with suspicion.Police had misused and abused their power, while at the same time the police measure of efficiency? clearance rates for traditional crimes? had never been impressive and did not improve. Similarly, the disclosures in recent years of crimes committed by the FBI as well as numerous police departments on both state and local levels had raised the specter of a police state. Taken together, these revelations have heightened the awareness of crime as well as added to the considerable skepticism about the established institutions of crime control.",
year =  1984,
