volume =  49,
number =  8,
pages = "2226-2244",
abstract = "This study offers a contextual analysis of female victims’ experiences with reporting incidents to the police in Trinidad and Tobago and highlights the implications for social work to support victims during the reporting process. Using a mixed-methods induction research design, thirty-one women victims of domestic violence were surveyed to find out their general perceptions and in-depth interviews were performed with eleven women to triangulate the study and who shared their interactions with the policing system. Findings show that the absence of structures, including the use of risk-assessment instruments to sustain the safety of the victims when making police reports and the pervasive patriarchal–cultural conceptions held onto by members of the police service regarding domestic violence, acts as a barrier to women from reporting abusive situations. The study also contextualises the cultural nuances experienced in living in a small island developing state and its ramifications for confidentiality and trustworthiness in the care and protection offered by the police to women. The findings call social workers to advocate for changes to unfair practices within policing systems and to improve delivery to adequately support the needs of victims even after the case has gone through the justice system. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)",
