@PHDTHESIS{Song2018-InformationNetworkModelQueryProcessing-..-kv,
title = "Information network model query processing",
author = "Song, Xiaopu",
publisher = "Carleton University",
abstract = "The accidents of Fukushima nuclear power stations produce too much of information about risk from nuclear power accidents. It generally assumed that after the people try to interpret the information by utilizing the specific mode of information process, they judge the risk. Our study will test the heuristic-systematic information-processing model (HSM) suggested by Shelly Chaiken. HSM suggested two information process modes in heuristic or systematic ways; the heuristic mode used the least amount of cognitive processing governed by availability, accessibility, and applicability whereas the system mode did the effortful processing, depending on comprehension, analysis and reliability of information. To test the workings of those two modes in judging the risk information related with Fukushima nuclear power accident, we will collect the data with survey method. The main topics in analysis will include as follow; first, examine the tendency of using two modes and relationships between those two modes; Second, analyze the impact of determinants of two modes on information processing, such as ability for judgment, motivation to make judgment, and information relevance; Third, investigate the contextual variables, e.g., information attributes or source credibility, to moderate the relationships between the determinant and two modes of information processing. Question \& Propose 1) Question A lot of previous studies in the information processing have focused on not only role of recipient's characteristics but also attributes of information and source's characteristics. However, there are few studies to integrate the recipient, information and source. Thus we elaborate this information processing model both by building the more comprehensive theoretical model and by testing it through empirical analyze of the survey data related with Fukushima nuclear power accidents. Especially, we investigate how the attributes of information and source's characteristics to moderate the relationships between the determinant and two modes of information processing. 2) Propose ① We examine the tendency of using two modes (heuristic-systematic information-processing) and relationships between those two modes in judging nuclear power accidents in Fukushima, Japan. ② We analyze the impact of recipient's characteristics, e.g., receipt's ability for judgment, motivation to make judgment, and information relevance, on using two mode information processing, ③ We investigate how the contextual variables , e.g., information attributes or source credibility, to moderate the relationships between the determinant and two modes of information processing. Theoretical Background Heuristic-Systematic Information Processing Model (HSM) assumes that heuristic and systematic information processing can occur simultaneously. Heuristic information processing depends on the availability, accessibility and reliability of judgmental heuristics. But systematic information processing is more effortful and capacity limited than heuristic processing. Heuristic information processing can proceed in either a more or less self-conscious manner. The principles of least effort and sufficiency are crucial motivational determinants of information processing mode (Eagly \& Chaiken, 1993, p. 341). Heuristic-systematic model Developed to apply to ``validity seeking'' persuasion settings in which people's primary motivational concern is to attain accurate attitudes that square with relevant facts In these settings, the primary processing goal of accuracy-motivated recipients is to assess to validity of persuasive messages, and that both heuristic and systematic processing can serve this objective (Chaiken, 1980, 1987) Research Model \& Data 1) Model We set the demographic variables, attributes of information, recipient's characteristics, source's characteristics as independent variables in explaining Dual Information Processing (Heuristic-systematic information processing) about nuclear power. As we state above, important determinants for Dual Information Processing (HSM) consisted of not only recipient's characteristic Variables but also attribute of information and source's characteristics. In this view point, it is important that we inspect for relationships between recipient's characteristics, attributes of information, and source's characteristics. Research Model of Dual Information Processing 2) Data We will use the survey data which are collected in 2012 with samples of 564. The following is a regression analysis on determinant of choice of systematic processing model against heuristic processing model, related with processing the information about nuclear power accident, Fukushima. As you can see from below Model 1, 2 and 3, demographical factor (gender and age), recipient factor (ability, motivation), source factor (credibility), information factor (ambiguity, usefulness, attraction) were showed the significant influences. Also in model 4, several interaction terms showed significant impact on HSM Findings 1 Below Table 1 clearly shows that each independent variables have a different impact on systemic information processing. Also it means that Dual Information Processing has different determinants. → Demographic : The male, the older show more systematic information processing than the female, the younger. → Recipient : The higher recipient's ability and motivation appear, the more systemic information processing occurs. → Source : More credible sources about information is, the more people process information systematically. → Information : More useful or interested information is, the more people process information systematically whereas more ambiguous information is, the more people process information heuristically. Hierarchical Regression Analysis In table 2, we analyze the correlation between HSM, systemic information processing, contrasted with heuristic one, and independent variables. The table show all of correlation coefficients significant. The relationship between HSM and Determinant variables Note: *. Correlation is significant at the 0.1 level (2-tailed) ;**. Correlation is significant at the 0.05 level (2-tailed) ; Correlation is significant at the 0.01 level (2-tailed) Findings 2 The ability, relevance, motivation, credibility, accuracy, usefulness, attraction has positive relation with systemic information processing. The ambiguity, however, represented opposite impaction (non-significant). It assumed that the attribute of information and source credibility intervene between receipts' motivation and choice of information processing model. So we will analyze the moderating effect. In Table1, Model 4 showed that that there are significant interactions between attributes of information, i.e, accuracy, usability, attractiveness of information, sources credibility and receipt's motivation. Moderating effects were presented in below Figures. Findings 3 Moderating effect exists between motivation and information usefulness. In other words, the information usefulness plays to suppress the effect of recipient's motivation on the systemic information processing. Summery Demographical factor (gender (-) and age (+)), recipient factor (ability (+), motivation (+)), source factor (credibility (+)), information factor (ambiguity (-), usefulness (+), attraction (+)) significantly influence the systemic information processing. Moderating effect by information usefulness exists, which attenuate the motivation's impact on the systemic information processing .",
year =  2018,
url = "http://dx.doi.org/10.22215/etd/2010-09023",
file = "All_Papers/30_ReviewE/340_Frauds/Song2015_XXXXX.pdf",
keywords = "340\_Frauds",
doi = "10.22215/etd/2010-09023"
}

@ARTICLE{Ross2014-ContraryToPsychologicalAndPopularOpinionThereIsNoCompellingEvidenceThatOlderAdultsAreDisproportionatelyVictimizedByConsumerFraud-..-xb,
title = "Contrary to psychological and popular opinion, there is no compelling evidence that older adults are disproportionately victimized by consumer fraud",
author = "Ross, Michael and Grossmann, Igor and Schryer, Emily",
journal = "Perspectives on psychological science: a journal of the Association for Psychological Science",
publisher = "SAGE Publications",
address = "Los Angeles, CA",
volume =  9,
number =  4,
pages = "427-442",
abstract = "According to psychological and popular opinion, older persons are especially susceptible to consumer fraud. Research on cognitive and affective aging reveals age-related changes that could increase the vulnerability of older persons to consumer fraud. However, this research does not show that consumer fraud actually is more prevalent among older persons. In generalizing from laboratory findings of cognitive decline to age differences in the prevalence of consumer fraud, psychologists may underestimate the influence in everyday life of possible protective factors associated with old age, including increased experience and changes in goals, lifestyle, income, as well as purchasing and risk behaviors. We review evidence on the prevalence of consumer fraud and conclude that there is no clear indication that it is more prevalent among older persons. Aggregating across all consumer frauds, there is evidence that consumer fraud is less common among older persons than adults of other ages. This evidence is potentially flawed, however, because of failings inherent in the methodologies. In the absence of irrefutable data, it is premature to conclude that consumer fraud is less prevalent among older adults, but it is also premature to conclude that consumer fraud is more prevalent among older persons, as is assumed in conventional and psychological wisdom.",
month =  jul,
year =  2014,
url = "https://www.jstor.org/stable/44290144",
file = "All_Papers/30_ReviewE/340_Frauds/Ross2014_16839.pdf",
keywords = "aging and decision making; cognitive aging; consumer fraud; emotional regulation and aging; successful aging;340\_Frauds",
doi = "10.1177/1745691614535935",
language = "en"
}

@ARTICLE{Reyns2013-OnlineRoutinesAndIdentityTheftVictimizationFurtherExpandingRoutineActivityTheoryBeyondDirect-contactOffenses-..-gp,
title = "Online routines and identity theft victimization: Further expanding routine activity theory beyond direct-contact offenses",
author = "Reyns, Bradford W",
journal = "The journal of research in crime and delinquency",
publisher = "SAGE Publications",
address = "Los Angeles, CA",
volume =  50,
number =  2,
pages = "216-238",
abstract = "Objectives: The purpose of the current study was to extend recent work aimed at applying routine activity theory to crimes in which the victim and offender never come into physical proximity. To that end, relationships between individuals' online routines and identity theft victimization were examined. Method: Data from a subsample of 5,985 respondents from the 2008 to 2009 British Crime Survey were analyzed. Utilizing binary logistic regression, the relationships between individuals' online routine activities (e.g., banking, shopping, downloading), individual characteristics (e.g., gender, age, employment), and perceived risk of victimization on identity theft victimization were assessed. Results: The results suggest that individuals who use the Internet for banking and/or e-mailing/instant messaging are about 50 percent more likely to be victims of identity theft than others. Similarly, online shopping and downloading behaviors increased victimization risk by about 30 percent. Males, older persons, and those with higher incomes were also more likely to experience victimization, as were those who perceived themselves to be at greater risk of victimization. Conclusions: Although the routine activity approach was originally written to account for direct-contact offenses, it appears that the perspective also has utility in explaining crimes at a distance. Further research should continue to explore the online and offline routines that increase individuals' risks of identity theft victimization.",
month =  may,
year =  2013,
url = "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/0022427811425539",
file = "All_Papers/30_ReviewE/340_Frauds/Reyns2013_16832.pdf",
keywords = "340\_Frauds",
doi = "10.1177/0022427811425539",
language = "en"
}

