abstract = "This paper comments on a study on the effects of administrative segregation reported by Ivan Zinger and his colleagues in the January 2001 issue of Canadian Journal of Sociology. The article by Zinger and his colleagues provides a review of the quantitative literature, reports the findings from an empirical study testing the effects of administrative isolation, and concludes that segregation for 60 days does not negatively prisoners' mental health and psychological functioning. The article makes a useful contribution to the debate by presenting fresh research on an important correctional issue, and also by focusing readers' minds on some of the methodological problems confronting researchers working in this area. Prisoners of correctional isolation are doubly outcast, and represent the ultimate in social exclusion: involuntary committals are first sequestered from the general population (in prison) and then isolated from the inmate community (in isolation). This paper explores some methodological and statistical problems and evaluates the validity of Zinger and his colleagues' conclusions about the effects of administrative segregation. Throughout this paper, the commenters use the phrase isolation interchangeably with segregation.",
