abstract = "Reviews the research concerning the feasibility of anti-shoplifting information programming, focusing on prevention through the mass media. It is argued that programs directly oriented toward juvenile shoplifters are likely to suffer problems in design and implementation; results of these programs are uncertain because of the complex psychological and social patterns characteristic of juvenile retail theft. Indirect deterrence programs aimed at the general public must overcome widespread apathy and ambivalence regarding appropriate behavioral and attitudinal responses before they can be effective. Evidence suggests that anti-shoplifting campaigns are more likely to modify attitudes than to affect behavior and that the organizational victims of shoplifting elicit a relatively low degree of public concern. It is concluded that crime prevention through mass media is a costly and difficult social undertaking that has limited applicability and only a moderate capacity for problem amelioration. (French abstract) (57 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2008 APA, all rights reserved)",
