number =  3,
pages = "326-327",
abstract = "Tests the assumption that public housing projects could be made more viable by affecting tenant well-being. 40 sociological and psychological subjective indicators of well-being were studied in 2 demographically similar urban public housing projects for the nonelderly. Female household heads from 84 of 210 units in Project 1 and from 126 or 288 units in Project 2 were interviewed. Responses were factor analyzed to uncover the dimensions of family well-being among the poor. 5 orthogonal dimensions emerged: livability of the project, identification with the project, fear of crime, social ties, and future aspirations. The 2 projects were distinct housing contexts with regard to livability, identification, and security. To determine whether tenant or project characteristics can predict these dimensions, multiple regression analyses using the 5 factors as dependent variables was employed. It was found that tenants who feel better off are older rather than younger, live in low-rise rather than high-rise projects, and report better maintenance and management of their projects. Implications of the results are noted. (30 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2012 APA, all rights reserved)",
