publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
address = "New York, NY, US",
pages = "xv-xvi",
abstract = "Study 1 tested whether alerting participants to relevant risk factors would reduce optimistic biases with 222 subjects (Ss). Study 2 tried to reduce optimistic biases by presenting risk factors in such a way that the 164 Ss would see their own attributes as less than ideal. In Study 3, 190 college students formed a mental image of someone at high or low risk for having a weight problem. Study 4 attempted to alter risk judgments by having 374 college students generate their own list of factors that might lead themselves to develop (or that would keep them from developing) a weight or drinking problem. These 4 studies, testing a variety of approaches and using a variety of health hazards, were unsuccessful in reducing optimistic biases about familiar health hazards. The findings strongly suggest that, at least for familiar health problems, reminding people of risk factors or encouraging people to compare themselves with a low-risk target has little impact on personal risk perceptions and does not reduce people's tendency to claim that they are less at risk than their peers. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2008 APA, all rights reserved) (from the chapter)",
