volume =  12,
number =  2,
pages = "79-107",
abstract = "Abstract A number of perspectives have been used to explain the nexus between community problems and the social and physical quality of the environment and its effects on people. Oscar Newman's seminal work in the area of the physical and social environment and the practice of informal control, has been primary in this regard. Moreover, there have also been analyses which have attempted to uncover the patterns of socio-economic and demographic “types” of people and their interactions within the community, and their possible influence on the phenomenon of social disorganization and crime (Greenberg, Rohe, and Wilhams, 1980; Newman and Frank, 1980; Smith, 1986; Figlio, Hakim, and Rengert, 1986). This analysis provides an additional integration of theoretical approaches in order to improve the assessment of social problems at the neighborhood level. More specifically, the research questions which are addressed in this study are: 1) What are the relevant dimensions or factors across different adjacent neighborhood sets which are indicative of social and physical characteristics of people and places? 2) Can crime be explained through a hypothesized model which integrates the concepts of defensible space, and social indicators? 3) Are there fluctuations in the hypothetical model based on the type of neighborhood, based on the crime rates, or within spatially bounded areas? The sample employed consists of three matched pairs of neighborhoods, which are similar in terms of socio-economic status, but differ in their crime rates. A total of 523 households were included in the study. The research questions are addressed in an exploratory factor analytic model in order to uncover the relevant dimensions in the social, physical, and demographic characteristics for each high and low crime neighborhood. Upon extracting a simple structure, a multiple regression model is to be applied both to explain and to assess the applicability and accuracy of the proposed model. The general implications of this study allow one to make conclusions about the nature of crime at the neighborhood level which may be valuable to both local and state agencies’ need assessments.",
