volume =  3,
number =  1,
pages = "28-42",
abstract = "Observed the theft of newspapers from in- and outdoor, free-access newspaper racks for 166 days during baseline and during the independent posting of 2 sign messages. One antitheft message was stated politely and appealed to internal, rule-governed control; the 2nd message was demanding and specified an aversive consequence for the undesired target behavior. Each sign was equivalently effective in reducing newspaper theft, increasing the mean percentage of potential money received for the papers by approximately 15\% and increasing the benefit–cost ratio from a substantial loss (during baseline) to a slight profit. There was no evidence of psychological reactance due to the threat appeal, and it is suggested that perhaps some theft due to psychological reactance was actually balanced by some theft deterrence due to an explicit statement of aversive consequences. Approaches to cost–benefit and cost-effectiveness analyses are compared, and directions for the future research of prompting strategies are discussed. (21 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)",
