number =  3,
pages = "243-250",
abstract = "Examined attitudes toward and beliefs about domestic violence in responses to open-ended questions from 37 hospital social workers, 69 family service social workers, 89 public welfare workers, 35 probation department workers, 55 nurses, 41 physicians, 121 police officers, 34 domestic-violence and emergency hotline workers, and 20 family court judges. Although differences were found among responses as a function of occupation and age, there was general agreement among Ss concerning both difficulties encountered by husbands and wives in preventing subsequent abuse and prevention strategies. Among difficulties cited as experienced by wives were lack of resources and services, fears, personality problems, stigma, communication dynamics, lack of support, guilt, and helplessness. Not admitting the problem and lack of willingness to work on it, lack of control and knowledge of alternative behaviors, habit, and stress were perceived as major difficulties encountered by husbands. Counseling was most frequently recommended for abusing husbands; counseling, separation/divorce, legal action, and nonprofessional help were most often recommended for abused wives. Although findings indicate substantial agreement among Ss regarding their perceptions of clients' problems and the solution of them, the extent of disagreement highlights the need for closer coordination and joint training of service providers. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)",
