abstract = "Despite the increased interest in risk communication among government agencies, there is evidence that agenciesâ€™risk communication practices lag. We conducted a study to explore which risk communication research would be most important to improve government agenciesâ€™risk communication practices. Qualitative interviews and a survey of 145 risk communication experts based in academic institutions and government agencies explored how important research on each of 48 topics would be to improving agenciesâ€™risk communication efforts. Respondents identified topics within three areas as priorities for further research: 1) involving communities in agency decisionmaking; 2) communicating with communities of different races, ethnic backgrounds, and incomes; and 3) evaluating risk communication. Both practitioners and researchers responded to additional statements about agenciesâ€™risk communication practices with reservations about staff and managersâ€™commitment to effective communication about environmental issues. We discuss the implications of these findings.",
