abstract = "Timothy Shilston is Chief Superintendent at Northumbria Police, UK. In his 2008 paper, the author concluded that quantitative measurements of police performance were flawed and that they should be replaced, or at least supplemented, by qualitative methods designed to analyse performance, and produce learning at an individual as well as organizational level. Following an air crash, investigators reconstruct the events which led to the failure in minute detail and plot them along a timeline. The resulting reconstruction of what happened is set alongside expectations of what should have happened at each stage, in order to establish the precise nature of the failings and to learn lessons for future application. By drawing on official records and interviewing service recipients, episodes of police service delivery can be analysed in a similar way, yielding valuable learning concerning the efficacy of organizational processes and the performance of individuals. Shortcomings in both can then be addressed, leading to higher standards of service delivery and, it is hoped, greater public confidence in policing. In this article, the author describes the qualitative assessment and performance improvement tool, which has now been piloted in a large English police force, the design of which draws on this analogy.",
