journal = "Journal of police science and administration",
volume =  5,
number =  3,
pages = "305-317",
abstract = "Examined the interconnections between public perceptions of the magnitude of the crime problem, fear of victimization, and social and demographic correlates of attitudes toward the police. Data were analyzed from 3,334 questionnaires obtained from a randomly selected multi-city sample of heads of households. The problem of crime was a major concern for 79.2\% of Ss and a significant majority expressed a fear of victimization. There was little evidence, however, that these concerns encouraged citizens to become hostile, antagonistic, or unsupportive of their respective police departments. Blacks, females, older citizens, and those in lower socioeconomic areas were significantly more concerned with the problems of crime than their counterparts; however, those most concerned were not the most likely to have actually experienced victimization. The most negative attitudes toward the police were reported by Blacks. Police images of public antipathy toward them are thought to be largely the product of their selective contacts with special categories of the public. Evidence indicates that the police themselves may have fostered negative attitudes by their responses to these citizens. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)",
