volume =  36,
number =  1,
pages = "16-48",
abstract = "Many studies have investigated social mechanisms’ effects on various crime prevention choices, while few have incorporated a disaggregation of home protection choices. Inspired by Yuan and McNeeley’s (J Crime Justice 39(4):455–472, 2016) individual-level crime prevention examination using singular measures, this study analyzes these measures’ concurrent use, offering a more robust analysis of home protection means. Survey data obtained from Seattle residents from 123 census tracts were analyzed using multilevel logistic regression, focusing on physical and social dimensions. The results revealed discrepancies between predictors and home protection behaviors (reaffirming Yuan and McNeeley) and similarities in individuals’ propensities to employ multiple home protection approaches. The study confirms homeownership’s influence on crime prevention choices and demonstrates the impact of living in areas of increased street codes on the use of some home protection measures.",
